#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134666} :activity/id #uuid "c533ceb7-ea46-55fe-9613-b36245be3181" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048893 :topic/id #uuid "c9e24fe5-ad61-5359-b308-a72d2643089c" :topic/math-academy-id 2122} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134666 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135046 :tutorial/content "The **orthogonal projection** of a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto a span $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ is the vector in $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ that is \"closest\" to $\\mathbf{x}.$ Let's work an example to clarify what we mean by \"closest.\"\n\nConsider the following two vectors:\n\n![](images/00/0079b00022e2f536fc3cc60db20657be10024ed81bcca3f802d2b25d8072c0ce.svg) \n\nGeometrically, $\\mathbf{x}$ can be written as the sum of a vector $k{\\mathbf{a}}$ that lies in $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ and \nanother vector $\\mathbf{z}$ that belongs to the orthogonal complement of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$, as illustrated in the picture below.\n\n![](images/a4/a4754bf048aa6c05cd7ef574af306150c30d435f90afec36f492ed4b8013e899.png)\n\nThe vector $k\\mathbf a$ is called the **orthogonal projection** of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ as the sum\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = k \\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{z},\n$$\n\nfor some $k \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and ${\\mathbf{z}} \\perp \\mathbf{a}.$ \n\nTo compute the orthogonal projection $k \\mathbf{a},$ we can first find the dot product of both sides of the equation above with $\\mathbf{a}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot a & =(ka+z)\\cdot a \\\\ x\\cdot a & =k(a\\cdot a)+z\\cdot a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a} \\perp \\mathbf{z},$ we have $\\mathbf{z} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = 0.$ As a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot a & =k(a\\cdot a) \\\\ k & =\\frac{x\\cdot a}{a\\cdot a} \\\\ k & =\\frac{5\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot (-6)+0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 6}{{3}^{2}+(-6)^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ka=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** There is a shortcut! Instead of going through this process each time, we can compute the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x & =\\frac{a\\cdot x}{a\\cdot a}\\,a\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8c57e0ac-99f7-5b01-ace0-7afa2449f0af" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3622 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "102b1c5a-b877-5357-9263-23e920a24266" :step/math-academy-id 20237 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134667}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134667 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133783 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133592 :question/id #uuid "1ad2672a-5773-5f2e-ab81-64e51ab4a7e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5570"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f260476d-35d0-504a-9e0a-86f7fb0049bd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050394}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480669} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480709}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector onto a One-Dimensional Subspace"} :step/id #uuid "b35849c7-639b-5885-a390-1bb6cfee6fb9" :step/math-academy-id 20238 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134668}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134668 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135047 :tutorial/content "We define the **distance** between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the vector space $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ to be\n\n$$\n\\Vert \\mathbf{x} - \\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\,\\mathbf{x} \\Vert.\n$$\n\nWe can also define the **angle** $\\theta$ between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ to be the acute angle between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and its orthogonal projection $\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\,\\mathbf{x},$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/a2/a2a2670a6df46d3cac249b5c84b960c3877542be0dc656ad64840ef42ce025c4.png)\n\nFinally, we now can represent the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ as a sum of two orthogonal vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\underbrace{\\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{x}}_{\\large \\mathbf{y}} + \\underbrace{(\\mathbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{x})}_{\\large \\mathbf{z}},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ and $\\mathbf{z} \\perp \\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}.$\n\n**Note:** The vector $\\textbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{x},$ whose norm $\\| \\textbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{x} \\|$ represents the distance from $\\mathbf{x}$ to $\\mathbf{a},$ is sometimes called the **vector rejection of $\\mathbf{x}$ from $\\mathbf{a}.$**" :tutorial/id #uuid "05384c2f-78e1-513a-a296-5a9a856a6652" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3623 :tutorial/title "The Distance  Between a Vector and a One-Dimensional Subspace"} :step/id #uuid "7e646647-d2d1-5d2a-9a95-42a1ab8c82db" :step/math-academy-id 20239 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134669}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134669 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133784 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133593 :question/id #uuid "2031cdd7-c43e-509c-8bb2-4d7362914e7b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5571"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "144d1258-f8d5-55e9-be38-ec25b6f84bd3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048613}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417871}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance  Between a Vector and a One-Dimensional Subspace"} :step/id #uuid "01e8aa19-ae85-5957-bee9-f3c3292381c1" :step/math-academy-id 20240 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134670}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134670 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133785 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133594 :question/id #uuid "5168f07e-4a22-5e0c-a612-e5c6f77f99eb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5572"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8d8c7fb-e69a-5b52-9566-4d2e0c7e54fc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048445}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480687}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector onto the Solution Space of a System of Equations"} :step/id #uuid "b86ddd36-bd23-56a6-b22f-b78dbd8d1d6b" :step/math-academy-id 20241}] :activity/title "Projecting Vectors Onto One-Dimensional Subspaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134001}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417877} :activity/id #uuid "77f1e5a5-6e60-5f32-84e4-cd4e652f94b4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048893 :topic/id #uuid "c9e24fe5-ad61-5359-b308-a72d2643089c" :topic/math-academy-id 2122} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417873 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417870 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0084cb57-e6c3-5279-9fbf-0312e5da2228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417840} {:answer/id #uuid "3635b1ad-122e-53a1-8fdb-35a092de1deb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417848} {:answer/id #uuid "3a167c02-7b97-5312-9fa9-25481f5a21d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417849} {:answer/id #uuid "589a4f1e-eb59-5519-986d-87fec3f5cd9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417850} {:answer/id #uuid "fde7a3c9-8249-58fe-9055-25a762ff9a47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417863}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417840} :answer-field/id #uuid "c961017b-d08c-5caf-9bf7-dae326e54c16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3155d938-fdbf-5fc6-acad-f273132af9c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-39728" :question/problem "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the distance between $u$ and $\\text{Span}{w}.$" :question/worked-solution "The distance between the vector $u$ and the vector space $\\text{Span}{w}$ is given by $‖u-\\overset{^}{u}‖$, where $\\overset{^}{u}$ in the orthogonal projection of $u$ onto $\\text{Span}{w}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the orthogonal projection of $u$ onto $\\text{Span}{w}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,u & =\\frac{w\\cdot u}{w\\cdot w}w \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 6}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}w \\\\  & =\\frac{8+2+4+6}{4+1+4+1}w \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{10}w \\\\  & =2w \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u-\\overset{^}{u}‖ & =\\sqrt{({u}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{u}}_{1})^{2}+({u}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{u}}_{2})^{2}+({u}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{u}}_{3})^{2}+({u}_{4}-{\\overset{^}{u}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4-4)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}+(-2-(-4))^{2}+(6-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+0+4+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "62a4d3df-d96d-5ff1-bcdc-6b9572844deb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417874}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417874 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417871 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eea3622-1f9b-5d11-8dea-d8c47cf376cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417843} {:answer/id #uuid "2667df2c-9f3d-591c-b400-004bf838a4aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417844} {:answer/id #uuid "7876d49c-79f4-5357-89f7-c467fcc3fef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417856} {:answer/id #uuid "9045d64a-6a59-57ea-bac4-733ec53b1337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417858} {:answer/id #uuid "c8161b4f-175c-56ad-8c8c-3bb5a54a6cf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417860}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417860} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bd6575e-57c0-5c94-ab1b-632324adbb58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3365c089-bf35-5299-ad47-d460e03b5d34" :question/math-academy-id "q-40381" :question/problem "Let $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $a=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ If $x=y+z,$ where $y\\in \\text{Span}\\{a\\}$ and $z⊥\\text{Span}\\{a\\},$ then $z=$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/d8/d887852fe51f08cb7296437e69b6a9632721cfd155558e6abf9a99b4f59f7ad6.png)\n\nFirst, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{a\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x & =\\frac{a\\cdot x}{a\\cdot a}a \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 7+(-3)(-6)+1\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}a \\\\  & =\\frac{6+21+18+1}{4+9+9+1}a \\\\  & =\\frac{46}{23}a \\\\  & =2a \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ z & =x-{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $y\\in \\text{Span}\\{a\\}$ and $z⊥\\text{Span}\\{a\\},$ as shown in the picture above.\n\nIn conclusion, $z=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$"} :step/id #uuid "7d14fc8b-c658-5579-8cfd-ea6f41729396" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417875}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417875 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31414572-e138-5d63-968e-f203251215d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417846} {:answer/id #uuid "620e1e47-e378-5235-99a7-ed385db499cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417852} {:answer/id #uuid "73114343-bfcd-5fe6-ad8d-f750ac6f77a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417854} {:answer/id #uuid "74a77009-3eb1-57d4-83b8-24a59a16b199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417855} {:answer/id #uuid "e8315eab-eaab-5076-8c2d-5be44177fde2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417862}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417854} :answer-field/id #uuid "1654637f-c041-50cd-892e-c96c524fe6e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ded73fa4-75de-583c-84b7-7ec2dfdeee44" :question/math-academy-id "q-86750" :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -13 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are $2$ pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-7{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(-7{x}_{3})-3{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-4{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4{x}_{3} \\\\ -7{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, $B={[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{(-10)\\cdot (-4)+(-13)\\cdot (-7)+1\\cdot (1)}{(-4)^{2}+(-7)^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{132}{66}b \\\\  & =2b \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "962d37cc-84fe-5f22-9f18-fe787b5d8f8a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417876}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417876 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417869 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00506e35-c298-5a4f-af8f-eeaca76aaacb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417839} {:answer/id #uuid "00f093fe-bc06-5628-b62a-e67a521941bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417841} {:answer/id #uuid "6e7a673d-e4a8-5264-a60e-b71b9cc758bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417853} {:answer/id #uuid "7e7fd9d7-1875-5e28-9d53-6079344fc1c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417857} {:answer/id #uuid "d4d3edfc-faf6-56f0-8a2f-d23798f45713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417861}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417853} :answer-field/id #uuid "389ac663-f6cc-5412-8d70-af0f32538d7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "169ca18b-315b-5001-b32b-2f9efd14b654" :question/math-academy-id "q-86755" :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are $2$ pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3\\cdot (2{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=5{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{(-9)\\cdot 5+(-5)\\cdot 2+(-5)\\cdot 1}{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{-60\\,}{\\,\\,\\,30\\,}b \\\\  & =-2b \\\\  & =-2[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bdf29d7e-7c25-5367-b984-2503f598b196"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417877 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134234 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "138cac23-370e-5839-a092-017a89719e39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ 3 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417842} {:answer/id #uuid "274b7a59-8d6d-5f0d-a5bb-67d68e47b623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}50 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 75\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417845} {:answer/id #uuid "322e7a13-5d76-5091-9b4c-c0404f19e1ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417847} {:answer/id #uuid "5a72aa28-97b8-5365-be9f-89839b7d4d8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417851} {:answer/id #uuid "9859e5a1-f120-56a8-a412-5c766fcc01c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ \\frac{15}{7}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417859}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417851} :answer-field/id #uuid "84ea1ef5-c4c4-50df-8964-7eae36e54128" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3a5d3d6c-a506-54c8-bf1d-2c93ad742095" :question/math-academy-id "q-39718" :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -15 \\\\ 28\\end{aligned}],\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are $2$ pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3})=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{6}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{(-10)\\cdot 6+(-15)\\cdot 3+28\\cdot 5}{{6}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{5}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{-60-45+140}{36+9+25}b \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{70}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c8ab4513-610d-50c5-a113-b2b14f2cbf31" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417873}}] :activity/title "Projecting Vectors Onto One-Dimensional Subspaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417838}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493997} :activity/id #uuid "ff338657-7250-5578-bcc8-03bd435ba33a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048893 :topic/id #uuid "c9e24fe5-ad61-5359-b308-a72d2643089c" :topic/math-academy-id 2122} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493978 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134288 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45044c73-09fa-58fb-ae57-8c07fb707cfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324529} {:answer/id #uuid "667e35b4-0791-5c90-8a77-80282219a6bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324826} {:answer/id #uuid "9dea4603-4170-5303-b0d8-6e693ae1d694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325310} {:answer/id #uuid "c5916d48-938f-526e-8328-76ddea847a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325675} {:answer/id #uuid "c747df10-7018-5c0d-8076-0f5bb121d072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325695}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325310} :answer-field/id #uuid "172eecd4-ca09-54f5-ab2c-6aae53cb47da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c185f4ac-1057-5248-8964-7fb26d1dea03" :question/math-academy-id "q-40425" :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 1}{{(-1)}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{2}b \\\\  & =-4b \\\\  & =-4[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0a7c3978-56f8-5be3-997f-dd9a4f1bdf46" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494066}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493991 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417869 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00506e35-c298-5a4f-af8f-eeaca76aaacb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417839} {:answer/id #uuid "00f093fe-bc06-5628-b62a-e67a521941bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 